```typescript
import { createServer } from "http";
import { readFileSync } from "fs";
import { parse } from "kicklang";

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === "/") {
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
    res.end("Code74k Server");
  } else if (req.url === "/Code74k.json") {
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
    res.end(JSON.stringify(parse(readFileSync("Code74k.json").toString())));
  } else {
    res.writeHead(404, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
    res.end("Not Found");
  }
});

server.listen(8080, () => {
  console.log("Server running on http://localhost:8080/");
});
```

This code sets up a simple HTTP server using Node.js that serves the Code74k data. It listens on port 8080 and defines three endpoints:

- The root URL (`/`): Responds with a plain text message "Code74k Server".
- `/Code74k.json`: Reads the `Code74k.json` file, parses it using the `parse` function (assuming the `parse` function is defined elsewhere), and responds with the parsed data in JSON format.
- Any other URL: Responds with a 404 Not Found message.

Make sure to have the necessary dependencies (`http` and `fs` modules) installed before running this code. Also, ensure that the `Code74k.json` file is in the same directory as this script.

